  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Power Africa:  U.S. International Development Finance Corporation (DFC)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Africa Regional):  These funds support Power Africa&apos;s efforts to meet the objectives of the Electrify Africa Act (Public Law 114-121) to increase access to affordable and reliable electricity in sub-Saharan Africa.  Specifically, the new U.S. Development Finance Corporation (DFC) will support the sourcing, review, execution, and monitoring of DFC financing and insurance transactions related to power sector projects in sub-Saharan Africa. These funds will be used to fund Washington-based deal teams, specializing in power project finance, who will focus their efforts to power project development in sub-Saharan Africa. To date, DFC (previously OPIC) has provided financing and political risk insurance to 33 different power projects in sub-Saharan Africa totaling more than $2.7 billion in development financing. . .DFC mobilizes private capital to help solve critical development challenges, advances U.S. foreign policy interests related to sustainable economic development in sub-Saharan Africa, and directly supports Power Africa&apos;s objective of increased access to reliable, affordable, and cleaner electric power. Because DFC works with the U.S. private sector, it helps U.S. businesses gain footholds in emerging markets, catalyzing revenues, jobs, and growth opportunities both at home and abroad. DFC achieves its mission by providing investors with financing, guarantees, political risk insurance, and support for private equity investment funds, and can support power projects in most countries in sub-Saharan Africa.. .DFC supports an economically viable form of private sector-led investment, offering a robust alternative to state-directed investment which often leaves countries saddled with debt. DFC achieves its mission by providing investors with financing, guarantees, political risk insurance, and support for private equity investment funds, and can support power projects in most countries in sub-Saharan Africa...</narrative>
  </description>
